a Kentucky Commission has voted to take  down a statue of Confederate President  Jefferson Davis from the State Capitol  it's part of a nationwide push to remove  symbols of racism and slavery and comes  in the heels of new legislation in  Louisville it bans police from entering  a suspects home without first  identifying themselves the law was named  Briana's law for Brianna Taylor who was  mistakenly gunned down by police back in  March  Jericka Duncan reports after heated  protests and emotional pleas Louisville  Mayor Greg Fischer signed  Briana's law under the new law police  will no longer be able to enter a  suspects home without announcing who  they are and they must wear body cameras  Taylor's mother Tameka Palmer it means  it was nearly three months ago March  13th  that police used a battering ram to  enter Taylor's apartment after midnight  believing it was being used by a drug  dealer to receive packages while the  plainclothes officers contend they  identify themselves Taylor's boyfriend  says they did not and he opened fire to  defend himself against what he thought  were intruders he later called 911 in  his arms after she was shot by police at  least eight times can you get her turned  over on her back there's been a dramatic  increase in the use of no-knock warrants  over the last 40 years mainly for  drug-related crimes with a nearly 7,000  percent increase sets Downton is a law  professor at the University of South  Carolina if officers aren't knocking and  announcing the individuals inside may  not know who is breaking down their door  which creates potential for error the  three officers involved in the shooting  death of Taylor have been put on  administrative leave while the  investigation is pending this week  authorities released a puzzling mostly  blank incident report on the shooting  that listed Brianna Taylor's injuries as  despite being shot at least eight times  by police the police department has  since acknowledged the error as for what  happens next to those three officers  involved in this case well that will be  determined by the state attorney general
 

